  "problem": "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9}{\u27e8g(t),g(t)\u27e9}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8g(t),g(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9}{\u27e8g(t),g(t)\u27e9}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{4}{5})}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
